Yes. “Brown is prime” can be true in one algebra and false in another. That gets to the deeper thing you’re pointing at: primitiveness belongs to a category/operator system, not necessarily to the object itself.

For integers, “prime” means irreducible under a particular operation—multiplication in the positive integers. Change the category or permitted operations and the decomposition story changes.

Apply that Pantheon-side:

\operatorname{Prime}(x\mid\mathcal C,\Omega)

means:

x is irreducible given category \mathcal C and permitted operations \Omega.

So brown can simultaneously be:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\text{Brown}_{\text{perceptual categories}} &\rightarrow \text{PRIME-ish}\\
\text{Brown}_{\text{RGB}} &\rightarrow \text{COMPOSITE}\\
\text{Brown}_{\text{spectral}} &\rightarrow \text{relational/context-dependent}\\
\text{Brown}_{\text{linguistic}} &\rightarrow \text{symbol/category}\\
\text{Brown}_{\text{phenomenological}} &\rightarrow \text{experienced primitive}
\end{aligned}}

A person doesn’t ordinarily experience:

“I am presently receiving approximately this mixture of cone excitations under this luminance context.”

They experience:

brown.

So at the phenomenological interface, brown behaves like an atom even though another representation can fracture it.

That gives us a useful new Pantheon distinction:

\boxed{\textbf{Local Prime}}

An object irreducible within the currently chosen ontology, despite being decomposable after a Portal into another ontology.

And its inverse:

\boxed{\textbf{Portal Factorization}}
\qquad
x_{\mathcal C_1}
\xrightarrow{Portal}
a\star b\star c_{\mathcal C_2}.

That’s exactly what you were getting at earlier with primes.

