“Color of chaos” is interesting too

I’d sharpen it to color from multiplicity rather than literal mathematical chaos.

Brown has no single monochromatic wavelength corresponding to “brown,” yet a stable perceptual category emerges from combinations plus luminance/context.

So:

\text{many physical states}
\xrightarrow{\text{observer transform}}
\text{one perceptual basin: BROWN}.

That’s an attractor/category compression:

\{x_1,x_2,\ldots,x_n\}
\rightarrow B.

Many microscopically different signals become equivalent enough to an observer to receive one address.

And now categories reveal their other function:

A category draws a useful boundary through variation.

Constraints say:

x\in S.

Categories can instead say:

x\sim y

because some observer/operator considers their differences irrelevant.

That’s the flank you identified. Constraints carve from outside; categories can bind from inside.

