And green makes this even clearer

“What color is the tree?”

Answer under one Portal:

\boxed{\text{green}}

Change the sense:

Human vision: green.

Spectrometer: spectral reflectance distribution.

Infrared sensor: an entirely different signature.

Photosynthetic machinery: not “green” at all—the important distinction is which photons are absorbed and usable versus reflected/transmitted.

Language: green, with cultural and categorical boundaries.

Physics: wavelength-dependent electromagnetic interaction with matter under particular illumination.

Same tree.

Different questions posed by different sensing operators:

S_i:X\rightarrow R_i(X).

So I think your sentence—

How you choose to describe green makes all the difference. And by what sense.

—contains a serious operator:

\boxed{
\text{Description}
=
\text{Object}
\times
\text{Sense}
\times
\text{Category}
\times
\text{Context}
}

Not multiplication literally; it’s the relational schema.

And sense should be generalized beyond biological senses.

A ruler “senses” length operationally.

A spectrometer senses spectral distribution.

A thermometer senses a temperature-correlated physical state.

A compiler senses syntax according to its grammar.

A market senses willingness to exchange through transactions.

An AI representation makes certain statistical/semantic distinctions available.

Each detector induces an ontology:

\boxed{
S
\rightarrow
\mathcal O_S
}

That’s an O-IOE mechanism.

Invent a new sense/operator S', and you may create distinctions unavailable under S:

\mathcal O_S
\xrightarrow{S'}
\mathcal O_{S+S'}.

New senses can create new addressable worlds.

Not necessarily new physical reality—new accessible partitions of reality.

And that brings us straight back to the Pattern Book and NTE: changing domain perspective is effectively giving the reader a temporary new sense organ.

Biology sees one decomposition.

Mathematics sees another.

Mechanics another.

Language another.

Code another.

Then ZPC asks:

\boxed{\text{What survives being sensed all those different ways?}}

That surviving kernel is a much stronger candidate for describing what the thing does or is structurally, while the differences tell us about the sensors themselves.

So I’d add two bricks to the Pattern Book:

\boxed{\operatorname{LocalPrime}(x,\mathcal O,\Omega)}

Irreducibility is ontology-relative.

And:

\boxed{\operatorname{SenseInducedOntology}(S)}

Every sensing/measurement/representation operator partitions possibility differently; changing the sense can expose previously inaccessible categories and factorizations.

Put them together and you get a beautiful Space Search maneuver:

When something appears primitive, don’t immediately break the thing. Change the sense with which you’re looking at it.

Then see whether the “prime” factors itself.